coverage. 66.5% on SWE-Agent but only 9.3% conf≥2 on OpenHands\n- Table 4: Gold stratified. conf=1 N=282 GB F1=0.66, MV Acc=0.71; conf≥2 N=223 GB F1=0.87\n- Table 5: Error decomposition on conf=1. GB-correct-MV-wrong=43, GB-wrong-MV-correct=49\n- Table 6: Human ceiling. κ=0.39, ceiling ~0.82, GB 0.839 (+1.9pp above ceiling)\n- Table 7: LLM judges on OH (N=2000). GPT-5.5 has 9.4% parse failure rate\n- Table 8: Calibration. GB gold ECE_raw=0.118, ECE_temp=0.058. Mid-range overconfident\n- Table 9: SHAP. Top 2 features (repeated_cmd_ratio, unique_file_edits) are LF-overlapping, 72.3% importance from LF-overlapping features\n- Table 10: Power analysis. conf=1 power=0.12, GB vs RF power=0.41\n- Table 11: Stride sensitivity. stride=5 CI width ~2x stride=1\n- Table 12: Early termination sweep. k=3 saves 13.1%, 83.4% task success\n- Table 13: Stagnation tail stats. 38.2% tailed, 18% waste steps, 15.9% waste tokens\n\n**Discussion:** Early termination is \"preliminary evidence.\" Circularity acknowledged: 72.3% SHAP from LF-overlapping features. Structural model ≈ 70% LF reconstruction + 30% residual.\n\n**Limitations (6 items):** Gold set power, scope (2 coding agents), 2-LF coverage collapse, encoder ceiling unknown, stride-1 autocorrelation, early termination simulation-only, calibration issues.\n\n---\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiments checklist)\n\nThe following experiments were planned. Check which are covered and which are missing:\n1. Compute Waste Audit → Table 13, §5.1\n2. Early Termination Simulation → Table 12, §5.1\n3. Cost-Effectiveness Pareto → §5.1 (AUC numbers mentioned)\n4. LLM-as-Judge on OH → Table 7, §4.7\n5. Conf=1 Error Decomposition → Table 5, §4.5\n6. BGE-M3 Probe → Table 2, §4.2\n7. E5-large Probe → Table 2, §4.2\n8. 2-LF Coverage → Table 3, §4.4\n9. Calibration → Table 8, §4.8\n10. Gold Power Analysis → Table 10, §4.11\n11. Human Ceiling → Table 6, §4.6\n12. Stride Sensitivity → Table 11, §4.12\n13. SHAP Feature Importance → Table 9, §4.9\n14. Trajectory Visualization → NOT in paper (missing)\n\n---\n\n## Your Review (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: experimental methodology, statistical rigor, comprehensiveness of ablations, honesty of reporting\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: methodological gaps, statistical concerns, missing experiments, circular evaluation, experimental design flaws\n\n### Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n### Preliminary Score (1-6) with justification\n\nBe rigorous. A top venue at 25% acceptance means scores of 3 (Weak Reject) or below are common for papers with significant methodological concerns. Pay close attention to:\n- The circularity problem (structural model trained on LF-derived labels, evaluated on LF-derived silver labels giving 0.995 F1)\n- The gold set's statistical power limitations\n- Whether the 0.967 F1 claim is meaningful given it's evaluated on silver labels\n- The autocorrelation issue with stride-1 windows\n- Whether the paper's claims are properly calibrated to its evidence\n","uuid":"95c28f15-91cd-453c-baea-1c8bdf8c8f7c","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Score on a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept), targeting ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Detecting Stagnation in LLM Agent Trajectories: An Empirical Analysis of Structural Features, Label Quality, and Deployment Trade-offs\"\n\nThe paper studies semantic stagnation detection in LLM agent trajectories (SWE-Agent 543 traj, OpenHands 3,409 traj) using weak supervision with 3 labeling functions (LFs: cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_bp). It compares structural classifiers (GB/RF on 30 handcrafted features), neural probes (Qwen3-0.6B, E5-large-v2, BGE-M3), and LLM judges (Claude variants, GPT-5.5).\n\n**4 Main Claims:**\n1. Label quality dominates transfer: fixing version-induced label bias on OpenHands yields +28pp improvement in probe fine-tuning\n2. Structural features offer Pareto-efficient deployment: 0.967 F1, <5ms, $0 cost vs best LLM 0.872 at $47/2K windows\n3. Temporal label models (TWV, HMM-TLM, Lag-aware HMM) all fail to beat majority vote — negative result\n4. Early termination shows limited potential: best saves 13.1% compute at 83.4% task success preservation\n\n**Gold validation:** 505 windows, 3 annotators, Fleiss κ=0.39, pairwise agreement ~80%. GB 0.839 vs MV 0.798 overall (p<0.001), but advantage concentrates on conf≥2 (Δ=+4.0pp, p=0.003); conf=1 inconclusive (Δ=−2.0pp, p=0.31). Human ceiling ~0.82 F1.\n\n**Key Tables:**\n- Table 1: Cross-agent transfer. Structural GB 0.967, Claude fs-CoT 0.872 (-9.5pp), BGE-M3 probe 0.780 (-18.7pp), Qwen probe 0.598 (-36.9pp)\n- Table 2: Encoder comparison. Gap narrows from 36.9pp (Qwen) to 18.7pp (BGE-M3)\n- Table 3: 2-LF coverage. 66.5% on SWE-Agent but only 9.3% conf≥2 on OpenHands\n- Table 4: Gold stratified. conf=1 N=282 GB F1=0.66, MV Acc=0.71; conf≥2 N=223 GB F1=0.87\n- Table 5: Error decomposition on conf=1. GB-correct-MV-wrong=43, GB-wrong-MV-correct=49\n- Table 6: Human ceiling. κ=0.39, ceiling ~0.82, GB 0.839 (+1.9pp above ceiling)\n- Table 7: LLM judges on OH (N=2000). GPT-5.5 has 9.4% parse failure rate\n- Table 8: Calibration. GB gold ECE_raw=0.118, ECE_temp=0.058. Mid-range overconfident\n- Table 9: SHAP. Top 2 features (repeated_cmd_ratio, unique_file_edits) are LF-overlapping, 72.3% importance from LF-overlapping features\n- Table 10: Power analysis. conf=1 power=0.12, GB vs RF power=0.41\n- Table 11: Stride sensitivity. stride=5 CI width ~2x stride=1\n- Table 12: Early termination sweep. k=3 saves 13.1%, 83.4% task success\n- Table 13: Stagnation tail stats. 38.2% tailed, 18% waste steps, 15.9% waste tokens\n\n**Discussion:** Early termination is \"preliminary evidence.\" Circularity acknowledged: 72.3% SHAP from LF-overlapping features. Structural model ≈ 70% LF reconstruction + 30% residual.\n\n**Limitations (6 items):** Gold set power, scope (2 coding agents), 2-LF coverage collapse, encoder ceiling unknown, stride-1 autocorrelation, early termination simulation-only, calibration issues.\n\n---\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiments checklist)\n\nThe following experiments were planned. Check which are covered and which are missing:\n1. Compute Waste Audit → Table 13, §5.1\n2. Early Termination Simulation → Table 12, §5.1\n3. Cost-Effectiveness Pareto → §5.1 (AUC numbers mentioned)\n4. LLM-as-Judge on OH → Table 7, §4.7\n5. Conf=1 Error Decomposition → Table 5, §4.5\n6. BGE-M3 Probe → Table 2, §4.2\n7. E5-large Probe → Table 2, §4.2\n8. 2-LF Coverage → Table 3, §4.4\n9. Calibration → Table 8, §4.8\n10. Gold Power Analysis → Table 10, §4.11\n11. Human Ceiling → Table 6, §4.6\n12. Stride Sensitivity → Table 11, §4.12\n13. SHAP Feature Importance → Table 9, §4.9\n14. Trajectory Visualization → NOT in paper (missing)\n\n---\n\n## Your Review (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: experimental methodology, statistical rigor, comprehensiveness of ablations, honesty of reporting\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: methodological gaps, statistical concerns, missing experiments, circular evaluation, experimental design flaws\n\n### Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n### Preliminary Score (1-6) with justification\n\nBe rigorous. A top venue at 25% acceptance means scores of 3 (Weak Reject) or below are common for papers with significant methodological concerns. Pay close attention to:\n- The circularity problem (structural model trained on LF-derived labels, evaluated on LF-derived silver labels giving 0.995 F1)\n- The gold set's statistical power limitations\n- Whether the 0.967 F1 claim is meaningful given it's evaluated on silver labels\n- The autocorrelation issue with stride-1 windows\n- Whether the paper's claims are properly calibrated to its evidence\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011px2qzXanuBtKfZzqbMU3j","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"0e1f76ac-958a-49af-adac-2972a650dab6","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:30:12.561Z"}
