{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U7q3Ymj8nDJL4aGhCUUVKN","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/acl.sty:309:  % This is necessary because \\href command is used in the acl_natbib.bst file.\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_latex.tex:20:% This is not strictly necessary, and may be commented out,\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_latex.tex:25:% This is also not strictly necessary, and may be commented out.\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl.sty:309:  % This is necessary because \\href command is used in the acl_natbib.bst file.\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_natbib.bst:381:FUNCTION {tie.or.space.prefix} % puts ~ before the preceding part if it is of length <3\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_natbib.bst:470:MACRO {dec} {\"December\"}\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_natbib.bst:1099:FUNCTION {archiveprefix.or.eprinttype} % holder for eprinttype with archiveprefix precedence\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_natbib.bst:1128:            { journal } % if journal field is not empty, output it (takes precedence)\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_lualatex.tex:8:% This is not strictly necessary, and may be commented out,\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/anthology.bib.txt:7:- `words` is the first significant word in the title, or more, if necessary,\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/README.md:29:make necessary changes. Minimally, you'll need to update the name of\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/formatting.md:69:Please make sure that your PDF file embeds all necessary fonts (especially for tree diagrams, symbols, and Asian languages).\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/formatting.md:237:If a piece of prior work appeared in multiple venues, the version that appeared in a refereed, archival venue should be referenced.\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/formatting.md:238:If multiple versions of a piece of prior work exist, the one used by the authors should be referenced.\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/formatting.md:296:Supplementary material may report preprocessing decisions, model parameters, and other details necessary for the replication of the experiments reported in the paper.\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:16:\\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document coding agent degradation; \\citet{chen2025monitoring} show sequence-level context is necessary; \\citet{kale2025weaktostrong} show weak monitors can oversee stronger agents; and \\citet{cemri2025multiagentfail,zhu2025agentfail} catalog systematic failure modes.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:147:Three CS graduate students annotate a stratified gold set; 512 windows received at least one annotation, with Set~B ($N{=}505$, ${\\geq}$2 annotators agree) as the primary evaluation (protocol and IAA in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_gold_set}).\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:24:Compared to trajectory-level labeling, windows provide the granularity needed to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a single task attempt, enabling downstream interventions such as early termination or strategy switching.\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:51:Uses the final task outcome as distant supervision: unresolved tasks' last windows receive stagnation votes; resolved tasks receive progress votes; all others abstain.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:310:Missed stagnation windows (FN) are not borderline cases---the model assigns them a mean stagnation probability of only 0.078 (i.e., high progress confidence of 0.922), with 73\\% receiving $>$0.9 progress probability.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:376:Of these, 512 received at least one annotation.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:379:Annotators receive the $k{=}5$ action descriptions with full command outputs and file diffs, but \\emph{not} the task outcome (resolved/unresolved), preventing information leakage from $\\lambda_3$.\ndocs/paper/acl_natbib.bst:381:FUNCTION {tie.or.space.prefix} % puts ~ before the preceding part if it is of length <3\ndocs/paper/acl_natbib.bst:470:MACRO {dec} {\"December\"}\ndocs/paper/acl_natbib.bst:1099:FUNCTION {archiveprefix.or.eprinttype} % holder for eprinttype with archiveprefix precedence\ndocs/paper/acl_natbib.bst:1128:            { journal } % if journal field is not empty, output it (takes precedence)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011px2qzXanuBtKfZzqbMU3j","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"74507db1-839a-4607-913e-585d7ce4afd0","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:30:41.405Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014fvorKMapoTJNygHN6C1mo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Jd5fi31Jun3QQkhFFUSeJ9","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Score on a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept), targeting ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Detecting Stagnation in LLM Agent Trajectories: An Empirical Analysis of Structural Features, Label Quality, and Deployment Trade-offs\"\n\nThe paper studies semantic stagnation detection in LLM agent trajectories using weak supervision across SWE-Agent (543 traj) and OpenHands (3,409 traj). It uses 3 labeling functions (cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_bp) to create noisy labels, then compares structural classifiers (GB/RF on 30 handcrafted features), neural probes (3 encoders), and LLM judges (Claude, GPT-5.5).\n\n**4 Main Claims:**\n1. Label quality dominates transfer: +28pp from fixing version-induced label bias\n2. Structural features offer Pareto-efficient deployment: 0.967 F1, <5ms, $0 cost\n3. Temporal label models fail (negative result)\n4. Early termination shows limited potential (13.1% compute saved, 83.4% task success)\n\n**Additional contributions claimed:**\n- First systematic quantification of stagnation's definitional ambiguity (κ=0.39)\n- Human performance ceiling (~0.82 F1)\n- SHAP analysis showing structural model ≈ 70% LF reconstruction + 30% residual signal\n- Three implications for weak supervision on sequential data\n\n**Related Work covers:**\n- Agent monitoring: CDCS/CDSA [Chen et al. 2024], SpecRA [Zhang et al. 2025], TrajAD [2025], ICLR Workshop [2025]\n- Process reward models: AgentPRM [Xi et al. 2026], PRM Framework [2025], ToolPRMBench [2026], Traj-RM [2026]\n- Self-reflection: Reflexion [Shinn 2023], LATS [Zhou 2024], ToT [Yao 2024], Weak-to-Strong [2025]\n- Weak supervision: Ratner et al. [2016], Safranchik et al. [2020]\n- Agent degradation: SlopCodeBench [2026]\n\n**Key numbers:**\n- Structural GB cross-agent: 0.967 F1 (but trained/evaluated on LF-derived silver labels)\n- Gold validation: GB 0.839 vs MV 0.798 (p<0.001), 505 windows, κ=0.39\n- Best LLM: Claude fs-CoT 0.872 on OH, gap 9.5pp vs structural\n- Best probe: BGE-M3 0.780 zero-shot, gap 18.7pp\n- 2-LF coverage on OH: only 2.7% at conf≥2\n- Early termination: 18% total waste, best k=3 saves 13.1% at 17% task success loss\n\n**Paper structure:** 11 pages, 13 tables, no figures. Introduction, Related Work, Method (problem formulation, LFs, detection approaches, TLMs, gold annotation), Experiments (11 subsections), Discussion (3 subsections), Limitations (7 items), Conclusion, References (17 entries).\n\n---\n\n## Your Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: novelty of problem framing, contribution relative to existing work, positioning within the field, intellectual honesty\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: novelty concerns, missing related work, overclaimed contributions, incremental nature, whether the \"first systematic quantification\" claim holds\n\n### Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n### Preliminary Score (1-6) with justification\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n\n**Novelty concerns:**\n- Cycle detection (CDCS) and process reward models already address aspects of agent progress monitoring. How different is \"semantic stagnation\" from what these capture?\n- The structural features (command repetition ratio, file edit counts) are straightforward engineering — is this a research contribution?\n- Weak supervision with labeling functions is well-established (Ratner et al. 2016). Applying it to a new domain without methodological innovation may be incremental.\n- The \"Pareto efficiency\" framing compares against LLM judges that are obviously expensive — is beating an LLM judge on cost a novel finding?\n\n**Related work gaps to consider:**\n- Is the related work comprehensive enough? 17 references seems light for an empirical analysis paper.\n- Missing: reward shaping for agents, online learning for trajectory monitoring, bandit approaches for resource allocation, RL-based early stopping\n- No discussion of concurrent work on agent evaluation frameworks\n\n**Contribution calibration:**\n- The paper honestly downgrades many claims (early termination is \"preliminary\", κ=0.39 is reframed as \"definitional ambiguity\")\n- But does honesty about limitations compensate for limited novelty?\n- The TLM negative result: is it because TLMs don't work here, or because the specific implementations were insufficient?\n- The label quality finding (+28pp): is this a contribution about stagnation detection, or a general finding about data quality that any practitioner would expect?\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14349,"cache_read_input_tokens":43765,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14349},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"d49d82bc-7b56-4f57-bbd5-7187a985e5ca"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"adfdca7752e8639b4","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jd5fi31Jun3QQkhFFUSeJ9","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Score on a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept), targeting ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Detecting Stagnation in LLM Agent Trajectories: An Empirical Analysis of Structural Features, Label Quality, and Deployment Trade-offs\"\n\nThe paper studies semantic stagnation detection in LLM agent trajectories using weak supervision across SWE-Agent (543 traj) and OpenHands (3,409 traj). It uses 3 labeling functions (cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_bp) to create noisy labels, then compares structural classifiers (GB/RF on 30 handcrafted features), neural probes (3 encoders), and LLM judges (Claude, GPT-5.5).\n\n**4 Main Claims:**\n1. Label quality dominates transfer: +28pp from fixing version-induced label bias\n2. Structural features offer Pareto-efficient deployment: 0.967 F1, <5ms, $0 cost\n3. Temporal label models fail (negative result)\n4. Early termination shows limited potential (13.1% compute saved, 83.4% task success)\n\n**Additional contributions claimed:**\n- First systematic quantification of stagnation's definitional ambiguity (κ=0.39)\n- Human performance ceiling (~0.82 F1)\n- SHAP analysis showing structural model ≈ 70% LF reconstruction + 30% residual signal\n- Three implications for weak supervision on sequential data\n\n**Related Work covers:**\n- Agent monitoring: CDCS/CDSA [Chen et al. 2024], SpecRA [Zhang et al. 2025], TrajAD [2025], ICLR Workshop [2025]\n- Process reward models: AgentPRM [Xi et al. 2026], PRM Framework [2025], ToolPRMBench [2026], Traj-RM [2026]\n- Self-reflection: Reflexion [Shinn 2023], LATS [Zhou 2024], ToT [Yao 2024], Weak-to-Strong [2025]\n- Weak supervision: Ratner et al. [2016], Safranchik et al. [2020]\n- Agent degradation: SlopCodeBench [2026]\n\n**Key numbers:**\n- Structural GB cross-agent: 0.967 F1 (but trained/evaluated on LF-derived silver labels)\n- Gold validation: GB 0.839 vs MV 0.798 (p<0.001), 505 windows, κ=0.39\n- Best LLM: Claude fs-CoT 0.872 on OH, gap 9.5pp vs structural\n- Best probe: BGE-M3 0.780 zero-shot, gap 18.7pp\n- 2-LF coverage on OH: only 2.7% at conf≥2\n- Early termination: 18% total waste, best k=3 saves 13.1% at 17% task success loss\n\n**Paper structure:** 11 pages, 13 tables, no figures. Introduction, Related Work, Method (problem formulation, LFs, detection approaches, TLMs, gold annotation), Experiments (11 subsections), Discussion (3 subsections), Limitations (7 items), Conclusion, References (17 entries).\n\n---\n\n## Your Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: novelty of problem framing, contribution relative to existing work, positioning within the field, intellectual honesty\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: novelty concerns, missing related work, overclaimed contributions, incremental nature, whether the \"first systematic quantification\" claim holds\n\n### Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n### Preliminary Score (1-6) with justification\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n\n**Novelty concerns:**\n- Cycle detection (CDCS) and process reward models already address aspects of agent progress monitoring. How different is \"semantic stagnation\" from what these capture?\n- The structural features (command repetition ratio, file edit counts) are straightforward engineering — is this a research contribution?\n- Weak supervision with labeling functions is well-established (Ratner et al. 2016). Applying it to a new domain without methodological innovation may be incremental.\n- The \"Pareto efficiency\" framing compares against LLM judges that are obviously expensive — is beating an LLM judge on cost a novel finding?\n\n**Related work gaps to consider:**\n- Is the related work comprehensive enough? 17 references seems light for an empirical analysis paper.\n- Missing: reward shaping for agents, online learning for trajectory monitoring, bandit approaches for resource allocation, RL-based early stopping\n- No discussion of concurrent work on agent evaluation frameworks\n\n**Contribution calibration:**\n- The paper honestly downgrades many claims (early termination is \"preliminary\", κ=0.39 is reframed as \"definitional ambiguity\")\n- But does honesty about limitations compensate for limited novelty?\n- The TLM negative result: is it because TLMs don't work here, or because the specific implementations were insufficient?\n- The label quality finding (+28pp): is this a contribution about stagnation detection, or a general finding about data quality that any practitioner would expect?\n","uuid":"eaa0b3d4-6406-4448-8c34-ebc2d07a6728","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Score on a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept), targeting ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Detecting Stagnation in LLM Agent Trajectories: An Empirical Analysis of Structural Features, Label Quality, and Deployment Trade-offs\"\n\nThe paper studies semantic stagnation detection in LLM agent trajectories using weak supervision across SWE-Agent (543 traj) and OpenHands (3,409 traj). It uses 3 labeling functions (cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_bp) to create noisy labels, then compares structural classifiers (GB/RF on 30 handcrafted features), neural probes (3 encoders), and LLM judges (Claude, GPT-5.5).\n\n**4 Main Claims:**\n1. Label quality dominates transfer: +28pp from fixing version-induced label bias\n2. Structural features offer Pareto-efficient deployment: 0.967 F1, <5ms, $0 cost\n3. Temporal label models fail (negative result)\n4. Early termination shows limited potential (13.1% compute saved, 83.4% task success)\n\n**Additional contributions claimed:**\n- First systematic quantification of stagnation's definitional ambiguity (κ=0.39)\n- Human performance ceiling (~0.82 F1)\n- SHAP analysis showing structural model ≈ 70% LF reconstruction + 30% residual signal\n- Three implications for weak supervision on sequential data\n\n**Related Work covers:**\n- Agent monitoring: CDCS/CDSA [Chen et al. 2024], SpecRA [Zhang et al. 2025], TrajAD [2025], ICLR Workshop [2025]\n- Process reward models: AgentPRM [Xi et al. 2026], PRM Framework [2025], ToolPRMBench [2026], Traj-RM [2026]\n- Self-reflection: Reflexion [Shinn 2023], LATS [Zhou 2024], ToT [Yao 2024], Weak-to-Strong [2025]\n- Weak supervision: Ratner et al. [2016], Safranchik et al. [2020]\n- Agent degradation: SlopCodeBench [2026]\n\n**Key numbers:**\n- Structural GB cross-agent: 0.967 F1 (but trained/evaluated on LF-derived silver labels)\n- Gold validation: GB 0.839 vs MV 0.798 (p<0.001), 505 windows, κ=0.39\n- Best LLM: Claude fs-CoT 0.872 on OH, gap 9.5pp vs structural\n- Best probe: BGE-M3 0.780 zero-shot, gap 18.7pp\n- 2-LF coverage on OH: only 2.7% at conf≥2\n- Early termination: 18% total waste, best k=3 saves 13.1% at 17% task success loss\n\n**Paper structure:** 11 pages, 13 tables, no figures. Introduction, Related Work, Method (problem formulation, LFs, detection approaches, TLMs, gold annotation), Experiments (11 subsections), Discussion (3 subsections), Limitations (7 items), Conclusion, References (17 entries).\n\n---\n\n## Your Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: novelty of problem framing, contribution relative to existing work, positioning within the field, intellectual honesty\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: novelty concerns, missing related work, overclaimed contributions, incremental nature, whether the \"first systematic quantification\" claim holds\n\n### Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n### Preliminary Score (1-6) with justification\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n\n**Novelty concerns:**\n- Cycle detection (CDCS) and process reward models already address aspects of agent progress monitoring. How different is \"semantic stagnation\" from what these capture?\n- The structural features (command repetition ratio, file edit counts) are straightforward engineering — is this a research contribution?\n- Weak supervision with labeling functions is well-established (Ratner et al. 2016). Applying it to a new domain without methodological innovation may be incremental.\n- The \"Pareto efficiency\" framing compares against LLM judges that are obviously expensive — is beating an LLM judge on cost a novel finding?\n\n**Related work gaps to consider:**\n- Is the related work comprehensive enough? 17 references seems light for an empirical analysis paper.\n- Missing: reward shaping for agents, online learning for trajectory monitoring, bandit approaches for resource allocation, RL-based early stopping\n- No discussion of concurrent work on agent evaluation frameworks\n\n**Contribution calibration:**\n- The paper honestly downgrades many claims (early termination is \"preliminary\", κ=0.39 is reframed as \"definitional ambiguity\")\n- But does honesty about limitations compensate for limited novelty?\n- The TLM negative result: is it because TLMs don't work here, or because the specific implementations were insufficient?\n- The label quality finding (+28pp): is this a contribution about stagnation detection, or a general finding about data quality that any practitioner would expect?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jd5fi31Jun3QQkhFFUSeJ9","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"9e86abbc-2320-4cab-a743-1bb32aca9df4","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:30:42.023Z"}
