{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Udsvct2oL4hDxsvnTjqtV3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Lf87iMTkmoRkB2SQRrJdJ8","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/method.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7137,"cache_read_input_tokens":9061,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7137,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jd5fi31Jun3QQkhFFUSeJ9","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"84eee3cd-cc0e-44d7-be6e-7b40f33c55e7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lf87iMTkmoRkB2SQRrJdJ8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\tWe frame stagnation detection as window-level binary classification over agent trajectories.\n5\tSilver labels are generated via weak supervision from three labeling functions, then used to train and evaluate three detection approaches: structural features, a neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline.\n6\tFigure~\\ref{fig:pipeline} provides a system overview.\n7\t\n8\t\\begin{figure*}[t]\n9\t\\centering\n10\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_pipeline.pdf}\n11\t\\caption{System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels).}\n12\t\\label{fig:pipeline}\n13\t\\end{figure*}\n14\t\n15\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n16\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n17\t\\label{sec:problem}\n18\t\n19\tGiven an agent trajectory $T = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n)$ where each $a_t$ is an action (e.g., a shell command, file edit, or API call), we partition it into overlapping sliding windows $w_i = (a_i, \\ldots, a_{i+k-1})$ with window size $k{=}5$ and stride 1.\n20\tThe task is to assign each window a binary label $y_i \\in \\{0, 1\\}$, where 0 denotes \\emph{stagnation} (the agent is stuck or unproductive) and 1 denotes \\emph{progress} (the agent is making meaningful headway toward the task goal).\n21\t\n22\tThe window formulation offers two advantages over alternatives.\n23\tCompared to step-level scoring, windows capture short-range temporal patterns---such as a sequence of repeated failed attempts---that individual actions cannot express.\n24\tCompared to trajectory-level labeling, windows provide the granularity needed to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a single task attempt, enabling downstream interventions such as early termination or strategy switching.\n25\t\n26\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n27\t\\subsection{Weak Supervision Pipeline}\n28\t\\label{sec:weak_supervision}\n29\t\n30\tPrior gold-standard annotations for agent stagnation at the window level are unavailable; we construct a stratified gold set (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}).\n31\tManual annotation is expensive: each window requires reviewing $k$ actions with full context (command text, outputs, file diffs), taking an estimated 2--3 minutes per window.\n32\tWe therefore adopt a data programming approach \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2019snorkel}: multiple labeling functions (LFs) provide noisy votes that are aggregated into silver labels.\n33\tEach LF $\\lambda_j : w_i \\to \\{0, 1, \\bot\\}$ examines a window and emits a vote: stagnation (0), progress (1), or abstain ($\\bot$).\n34\t\n35\t\\subsubsection{Labeling Functions}\n36\t\\label{sec:labeling_functions}\n37\t\n38\tWe design three LFs that target complementary signals of stagnation.\n39\t\n40\t\\paragraph{Command repetition ($\\lambda_1$).}\n41\tComputes the proportion of repeated commands within $w_i$; a high ratio triggers a stagnation vote.\n42\tNever abstains.\n43\tFires on 65.5\\% of SWE-Agent windows but only 5.4\\% of OH windows (97.9\\% precision), reflecting agent-specific temporal dynamics (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n44\t\n45\t\\paragraph{File diff delta ($\\lambda_2$).}\n46\tMeasures the fraction of steps producing code modifications.\n47\tLow modification frequency triggers stagnation; ${>}1$ edited file triggers progress; otherwise abstains.\n48\tOn OH, achieves 87.5\\% recall (99.9\\% precision), the most reliable cross-coding-agent signal.\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{Outcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$).}\n51\tUses the final task outcome as distant supervision: unresolved tasks' last windows receive stagnation votes; resolved tasks receive progress votes; all others abstain.\n52\tUnlike $\\lambda_1$/$\\lambda_2$, $\\lambda_3$ propagates a global signal backward through time.\n53\t\n54\t\n55\t\\subsubsection{Label Aggregation}\n56\t\\label{sec:label_aggregation}\n57\t\n58\tSilver labels are produced by majority vote among non-abstaining LFs:\n59\t\\begin{equation}\n60\ty_i = \\text{MV}\\bigl(\\{\\lambda_j(w_i) : \\lambda_j(w_i) \\neq \\bot\\}\\bigr)\n61\t\\label{eq:mv}\n62\t\\end{equation}\n63\tWe define the confidence of each label as the number of non-abstaining LFs that agree with the winning label: $\\text{conf}_i = |\\{j : \\lambda_j(w_i) \\neq \\bot \\;\\text{and}\\; \\lambda_j(w_i) = y_i\\}| \\in \\{1, 2, 3\\}$.\n64\tConf${=}1$ arises when a single non-abstaining LF determines the label---either because only one LF votes or because two LFs disagree with no clear majority---producing the most ambiguous labels.\n65\tThe conf${\\geq}$2 subset, where at least two non-abstaining LFs agree on the winning label, serves as the primary evaluation set throughout this work.\n66\tThis stratification allows us to separately analyze model behavior on high-confidence vs.\\ ambiguous windows (\\S\\ref{sec:main_results}).\n67\t\n68\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n69\t\\subsection{Detection Methods}\n70\t\\label{sec:detection_methods}\n71\t\n72\t\\subsubsection{Structural Features}\n73\t\\label{sec:structural_features}\n74\t\n75\tWe extract a 30-dimensional feature vector from each window, requiring no pretrained model or GPU.\n76\tFeatures span four categories:\n77\t(1)~\\emph{repetition}: exact command repeat ratio, fuzzy match ratio (Levenshtein-based), and unique command ratio;\n78\t(2)~\\emph{file operations}: counts of file creates, modifications, and deletions within the window;\n79\t(3)~\\emph{edit patterns}: edit distances between consecutive actions, capturing how much the agent's behavior changes step-to-step;\n80\t(4)~\\emph{output statistics}: entropy of command outputs and action type distribution features.\n81\tThese features are fed to three classifiers: Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).\n82\tBecause several structural features overlap with the signals used by the labeling functions (e.g., repetition ratios relate to $\\lambda_1$), structural classifiers can partially reconstruct the LF voting process.\n83\tWe discuss this circularity and its implications in \\S\\ref{sec:main_results}.\n84\t\n85\t\\subsubsection{NL Embedding Probe (ProgressProbe)}\n86\t\\label{sec:progressprobe}\n87\t\n88\tEach action $a_t$ is encoded as a natural language description and embedded using Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \\citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} into a 1024-dimensional vector.\n89\tThe $k$ embeddings within a window form a sequence that is processed by a 2-layer GRU \\citep{cho2014gru} with hidden size 256 and dropout 0.1.\n90\tAn attention mechanism \\citep{bahdanau2015attention} computes a weighted combination of the GRU hidden states, yielding a single window-level representation.\n91\tThis representation is passed through a fully connected layer with sigmoid activation for binary classification.\n92\tTraining uses binary cross-entropy loss with early stopping (patience 5) on a validation set.\n93\t\n94\tThe GRU architecture is motivated by the sequential nature of actions within a window: the order in which commands are issued (e.g., repeated failures followed by a different approach) carries diagnostic information that bag-of-embeddings methods discard.\n95\tWe also evaluate two simpler embedding baselines to isolate the contribution of temporal modeling: (1)~mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression, and (2)~cosine similarity between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.\n96\t\n97\t\\subsubsection{LLM-as-Judge Baseline}\n98\t\\label{sec:llm_judge}\n99\t\n100\tWe prompt Claude Sonnet 4.6 in zero-shot mode to classify each window as stagnation or progress, given the textual descriptions of actions within the window.\\footnote{Extended LLM evaluation including chain-of-thought prompting, few-shot examples, and multi-model comparison is reported in Appendix~\\ref{sec:llm_extended}.}\n101\tThe LLM judge establishes a cost-performance reference: at \\$11.66 per test set, its zero-shot F1 of 0.663 demonstrates that stagnation detection is non-trivial even for capable LLMs without task-specific adaptation.\n102\tThis baseline is not used in the silver label pipeline.\n103\t\n104\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n105\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\n106\t\\label{sec:temporal_models}\n107\t\n108\tThe three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches relative to stagnation onset (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}): $\\lambda_1$ leads by ${\\sim}2$ steps, $\\lambda_2$ is near-concurrent, and $\\lambda_3$ lags by ${\\sim}7$ steps.\n109\tThis motivates testing whether temporal aggregation improves over majority vote.\n110\tWe evaluate three temporal label models---Time-Varying Weights (TWV) with Gaussian kernel smoothing, a Hidden Markov Model (HMM) \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm} over joint LF patterns, and a Lag-Aware HMM that models each LF's temporal offset---alongside two generative i.i.d.\\ models: FlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid} and Snorkel MeTaL \\citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal}.\n111\tFull model specifications appear in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_temporal_models}."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jd5fi31Jun3QQkhFFUSeJ9","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"b8d8bb74-61f5-4ba4-a164-352d20ae4ec6","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:30:46.549Z"}
