{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012m7956dtqUskU5sQWpeYTE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Supplementary Results}\n2\t\\label{sec:appendix}\n3\t\n4\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n5\t\\subsection{Encoder Ablation}\n6\t\\label{sec:app_encoder}\n7\t\n8\t\\begin{table}[h]\n9\t\\centering\n10\t\\small\n11\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n12\t\\toprule\n13\t\\textbf{Encoder} & \\textbf{Dim} & \\textbf{Macro-F1} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{stag}} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{prog}} & \\textbf{Best Ep.} \\\\\n14\t\\midrule\n15\tQwen3-0.6B & 1024 & 0.9055 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\n16\tE5-large-v2 & 1024 & 0.9363 & 0.9067 & 0.9660 & 4 \\\\\n17\tBGE-M3 & 1024 & \\textbf{0.9413} & \\textbf{0.9147} & \\textbf{0.9679} & 5 \\\\\n18\t\\bottomrule\n19\t\\end{tabular}\n20\t\\caption{Encoder ablation on SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 ($N{=}1{,}824$). All models use the same GRU architecture (2-layer, hidden$=$256, dropout$=$0.1, lr$=$1e-3, batch$=$32, early stopping patience~5). All encoders exceed 0.90 macro-F1, confirming the probe's temporal architecture drives performance rather than a specific encoder. BGE-M3 achieves the highest score ($+$3.6\\,pp over Qwen3), but the gap between encoders (0.5\\,pp) is small relative to the structural--probe gap (8.96\\,pp). ``---'' for Qwen3 indicates per-class scores were not logged in the initial sweep; macro-F1 is computed from the checkpoint with lowest validation loss.}\n21\t\\label{tab:encoder_ablation}\n22\t\\end{table}\n23\t\n24\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n25\t\\subsection{Window Size Ablation}\n26\t\\label{sec:app_window}\n27\t\n28\t\\begin{table}[h]\n29\t\\centering\n30\t\\small\n31\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n32\t\\toprule\n33\t\\textbf{Window $k$} & \\textbf{Macro-F1} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{stag}} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{prog}} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs.\\ $k{=}5$} \\\\\n34\t\\midrule\n35\t3 & 0.8721 & 0.7784 & 0.9659 & $-$5.9\\,pp \\\\\n36\t\\textbf{5} & \\textbf{0.9309} & \\textbf{0.8917} & \\textbf{0.9701} & --- \\\\\n37\t7 & 0.9133 & 0.8791 & 0.9475 & $-$1.8\\,pp \\\\\n38\t\\bottomrule\n39\t\\end{tabular}\n40\t\\caption{Window size ablation on SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 using the Qwen3-0.6B encoder (seed~42). $k{=}5$ is optimal. $k{=}3$ lacks sufficient context for stagnation detection (F1\\textsubscript{stag} drops 11.3\\,pp). $k{=}7$ dilutes signal by spanning both progress and stagnation phases.}\n41\t\\label{tab:window_ablation}\n42\t\\end{table}\n43\t\n44\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n45\t\\subsection{Multi-Seed Stability}\n46\t\\label{sec:app_multiseed}\n47\t\n48\t\\begin{table}[h]\n49\t\\centering\n50\t\\small\n51\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n52\t\\toprule\n53\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{In-Domain F1} & \\textbf{Seeds} & \\textbf{Cross-Agent F1} & \\textbf{Seeds} \\\\\n54\t\\midrule\n55\tStructural RF & $0.9951 \\pm 0.0000$ & 5 & --- & --- \\\\\n56\tStructural GB & --- & --- & $0.9678 \\pm 0.0015$ & 5 \\\\\n57\tProgressProbe (GRU) & $0.9185 \\pm 0.0113$ & 5 & --- & --- \\\\\n58\t\\bottomrule\n59\t\\end{tabular}\n60\t\\caption{Multi-seed stability (mean $\\pm$ std). In-domain: SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2; cross-coding-agent: SWE-Agent $\\to$ OpenHands zero-shot transfer. Structural classifiers show zero or near-zero variance. The probe exhibits higher variance ($\\sigma{=}0.011$) but all seeds exceed 0.90. ``---'' indicates the setting was not evaluated: RF is evaluated in-domain only, GB cross-coding-agent only, and the probe in-domain only.}\n61\t\\label{tab:multiseed}\n62\t\\end{table}\n63\t\n64\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n65\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Label Quality and Few-Shot Transfer}\n66\t\\label{sec:app_cmdrep}\n67\t\n68\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_agent_cmdrep} extends the label quality analysis from \\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}, showing probe fine-tuning performance across data fractions under corrected OH labels.\n69\t\n70\t\\begin{table}[h]\n71\t\\centering\n72\t\\small\n73\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n74\t\\toprule\n75\t\\textbf{OH Data Fraction} & \\textbf{Probe Macro-F1} \\\\\n76\t\\midrule\n77\t0\\% (zero-shot) & 0.598 \\\\\n78\t5\\% & 0.850 \\\\\n79\t10\\% & 0.918 \\\\\n80\t20\\% & 0.948 \\\\\n81\t50\\% & 0.968 \\\\\n82\t100\\% & 0.976 \\\\\n83\t\\bottomrule\n84\t\\end{tabular}\n85\t\\caption{Probe fine-tuning on corrected OH labels across data fractions. The zero-shot--to--5\\% jump ($+$25.2\\,pp) and rapid convergence toward structural GB (0.992 on conf${\\geq}$2) demonstrate that label quality, not model capacity, was the transfer bottleneck. Under old (corrupted) labels, 5\\% fine-tuning reached only 0.570.}\n86\t\\label{tab:cross_agent_cmdrep}\n87\t\\end{table}\n88\t\n89\tThe probe reaches 0.948 macro-F1 at 20\\% OH data---within 5\\,pp of structural GB on conf${\\geq}$2 windows (0.992)---and at 50\\% (0.968) closes to within 2.5\\,pp of structural GB on conf${\\geq}$2 (0.992).\n90\tThis rapid convergence under corrected labels contrasts sharply with the old label regime, where even 100\\% fine-tuning reached only 0.751.\n91\tThe improvement is driven entirely by label quality: the model architecture and training procedure are identical.\n92\t\n93\tUnder the prevalence-matched ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}), 3-LF and 2-LF label sets agree on 100\\% of overlapping windows, confirming that cmd\\_rep's contribution is coverage (expanding conf${\\geq}$2 training data from ${\\sim}$4K to ${\\sim}$43K windows) rather than label refinement.\n94\tThe $-29.4$\\,pp drop upon removing cmd\\_rep (McNemar $\\chi^2{=}1{,}551$, $p{\\approx}0$) is thus attributable to data volume collapse, not label noise.\n95\t\n96\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n97\t\\subsection{Structural Feature Definitions}\n98\t\\label{sec:app_features}\n99\t\n100\tTable~\\ref{tab:feature_defs} lists all 30 structural features with their categories and LF overlap classification.\n101\tA feature is classified as LF-overlapping if it is computed from the same signal space that any labeling function uses as input (\\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap}).\n102\t\n103\t\\begin{table*}[h]\n104\t\\centering\n105\t\\small\n106\t\\begin{tabular}{rlll}\n107\t\\toprule\n108\t\\textbf{\\#} & \\textbf{Feature} & \\textbf{Category} & \\textbf{LF Overlap} \\\\\n109\t\\midrule\n110\t1  & num\\_steps             & Trajectory stats    & --- \\\\\n111\t2  & num\\_actions           & Trajectory stats    & --- \\\\\n112\t3  & num\\_observations      & Trajectory stats    & --- \\\\\n113\t4  & action\\_ratio          & Trajectory stats    & --- \\\\\n114\t5  & mean\\_text\\_len        & Text statistics     & --- \\\\\n115\t6  & std\\_text\\_len         & Text statistics     & --- \\\\\n116\t7  & max\\_text\\_len         & Text statistics     & --- \\\\\n117\t8  & min\\_text\\_len         & Text statistics     & --- \\\\\n118\t9  & mean\\_action\\_len      & Text statistics     & --- \\\\\n119\t10 & mean\\_obs\\_len         & Text statistics     & --- \\\\\n120\t11 & text\\_len\\_trend       & Text statistics     & --- \\\\\n121\t12 & num\\_commands          & Command stats       & --- \\\\\n122\t13 & mean\\_cmd\\_len         & Command stats       & --- \\\\\n123\t14 & max\\_cmd\\_len          & Command stats       & --- \\\\\n124\t15 & unique\\_cmd\\_ratio     & Repetition          & $\\lambda_1$ \\\\\n125\t16 & max\\_repeat\\_ratio     & Repetition          & $\\lambda_1$ \\\\\n126\t17 & num\\_edit\\_cmds        & File operations     & $\\lambda_2$ \\\\\n127\t18 & num\\_search\\_cmds      & File operations     & $\\lambda_2$ \\\\\n128\t19 & num\\_nav\\_cmds         & File operations     & --- \\\\\n129\t20 & num\\_submit\\_cmds      & File operations     & --- \\\\\n130\t21 & edit\\_ratio            & File operations     & $\\lambda_2$ \\\\\n131\t22 & search\\_ratio          & File operations     & $\\lambda_2$ \\\\\n132\t23 & avg\\_adjacent\\_cmd\\_sim & Edit distance       & $\\lambda_1$ \\\\\n133\t24 & max\\_adjacent\\_cmd\\_sim & Edit distance       & $\\lambda_1$ \\\\\n134\t25 & max\\_consec\\_similar   & Edit distance       & $\\lambda_1$ \\\\\n135\t26 & num\\_errors            & Output statistics   & --- \\\\\n136\t27 & num\\_test\\_outputs     & Output statistics   & --- \\\\\n137\t28 & num\\_file\\_displays    & Output statistics   & $\\lambda_2$ \\\\\n138\t29 & num\\_nonzero\\_exits    & Output statistics   & --- \\\\\n139\t30 & is\\_short\\_trajectory  & Trajectory stats    & $\\lambda_3$ \\\\\n140\t\\bottomrule\n141\t\\end{tabular}\n142\t\\caption{Complete structural feature definitions ($N{=}30$). LF Overlap indicates which labeling function's signal space the feature shares: $\\lambda_1$ (command repetition), $\\lambda_2$ (file diff), $\\lambda_3$ (outcome backpropagation), or ``---'' (no overlap). 11 features overlap with at least one LF; 19 are residual.}\n143\t\\label{tab:feature_defs}\n144\t\\end{table*}\n145\t\n146\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n147\t\\subsection{Instance Isolation}\n148\t\\label{sec:app_instance_isolation}\n149\t\n150\t\\begin{table}[h]\n151\t\\centering\n152\t\\small\n153\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n154\t\\toprule\n155\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Full} & \\textbf{Unseen-only} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n156\t\\midrule\n157\tGB all & 0.9674 & 0.9669 & $-$0.05\\,pp \\\\\n158\tRF all & 0.9123 & 0.9121 & $-$0.01\\,pp \\\\\n159\tGB conf${\\geq}$2 & 0.9919 & 0.9917 & $-$0.02\\,pp \\\\\n160\tRF conf${\\geq}$2 & 0.9959 & 0.9962 & $+$0.03\\,pp \\\\\n161\t\\bottomrule\n162\t\\end{tabular}\n163\t\\caption{Instance isolation: cross-coding-agent macro-F1 with and without 284 SWE-bench task instances that overlap between the 1,386 SWE-bench training instances and 3,409 OH test instances (8.3\\% of OH). All $|\\Delta| < 0.1$\\,pp, confirming transfer reflects behavioral pattern generalization rather than instance memorization.}\n164\t\\label{tab:instance_isolation}\n165\t\\end{table}\n166\t\n167\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n168\t\\subsection{Extended LLM-as-Judge Evaluation}\n169\t\\label{sec:llm_extended}\n170\t\n171\tTable~\\ref{tab:llm_extended} extends the zero-shot LLM-as-judge baseline (Table~\\ref{tab:main_results}) with chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting, few-shot examples, and an alternative model, all evaluated on the SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set ($N{=}1{,}824$).\n172\t\n173\t\\begin{table}[h]\n174\t\\centering\n175\t\\small\n176\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n177\t\\toprule\n178\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Setting} & \\textbf{Macro-F1} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{stag}} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{prog}} & \\textbf{Cost} \\\\\n179\t\\midrule\n180\tClaude Sonnet 4.6 & zero-shot & 0.6625 & 0.6055 & 0.7195 & \\$11.66 \\\\\n181\tClaude Sonnet 4.6 & CoT & 0.7499 & 0.6808 & 0.8189 & \\$17.16 \\\\\n182\tClaude Sonnet 4.6 & 5-shot & 0.7514 & 0.6828 & 0.8199 & \\$36.08 \\\\\n183\tClaude Sonnet 4.6 & 5-shot-CoT & \\textbf{0.7994} & \\textbf{0.7306} & \\textbf{0.8681} & \\$40.56 \\\\\n184\tGPT-5.5$^\\dagger$ & zero-shot & 0.7465 & 0.7110 & 0.7819 & \\$11.62 \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\emph{Structural feature augmentation}} \\\\\n187\tClaude Sonnet 4.6 & + structural input$^\\ddagger$ & 0.6880 & 0.6270 & 0.7490 & \\$14.82 \\\\\n188\t\\bottomrule\n189\t\\end{tabular}\n190\t\\caption{Extended LLM-as-judge evaluation on SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 ($N{=}1{,}824$). All Claude rows use the same model; the performance gain comes from prompting strategy. CoT alone yields gains comparable to few-shot ($+$8.7\\,pp vs.\\ $+$8.9\\,pp over zero-shot); combining them (five-shot-CoT) adds a further 4.8\\,pp, suggesting complementary mechanisms. $^\\ddagger$30 structural features provided as formatted text in the LLM prompt; performance degrades below pure few-shot (0.751), indicating structural signals require engineered classifiers. $^\\dagger$GPT-5.5: 22.4\\% of responses (408/1,824) failed to parse; macro-F1 is computed over the 1,416 windows with valid outputs. The best LLM configuration (0.799 in-domain) trails structural cross-coding-agent transfer (0.967) by 16.8\\,pp. Total API cost across all configurations: \\$131.90.}\n191\t\\label{tab:llm_extended}\n192\t\\end{table}\n193\t\n194\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n195\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Model Specifications}\n196\t\\label{sec:app_temporal_models}\n197\t\n198\t\\subsubsection{Time-Varying Weights (TWV)}\n199\t\n200\tTWV applies Gaussian kernel smoothing to each LF's activation sequence:\n201\t\\begin{equation}\n202\t\\tilde{\\lambda}_j(t) = \\sum_{t'} K_\\sigma(t - t') \\cdot \\lambda_j(t')\n203\t\\label{eq:twv}\n204\t\\end{equation}\n205\twhere $K_\\sigma$ is a Gaussian kernel with bandwidth $\\sigma$.\n206\tSmoothed activations replace raw votes for conf${=}1$ windows (where exactly one LF disagrees), potentially flipping labels in temporally ambiguous regions.\n207\tThe bandwidth $\\sigma$ controls the extent of temporal context: larger values incorporate more distant steps.\n208\t\n209\t\\subsubsection{Hidden Markov Models}\n210\t\n211\tWe model the label sequence as a Hidden Markov Model \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm} with two hidden states (stagnation, progress) and observations defined by the joint activation pattern of the three LFs.\n212\tTransition and emission parameters are estimated via Expectation-Maximization (EM).\n213\tThe HMM captures temporal persistence---stagnation episodes tend to span multiple consecutive windows---which majority vote, operating independently per window, cannot model.\n214\t\n215\t\\subsubsection{Lag-Aware HMM}\n216\t\n217\tThe standard HMM treats all LFs as synchronous.\n218\tLag-Aware HMM extends this by explicitly modeling each LF's temporal offset: $\\lambda_1$ is shifted by $-2$ steps, $\\lambda_2$ by $-1$, and $\\lambda_3$ by $+7$, estimated from the temporal dynamics analysis (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}).\n219\tTo prevent divergence, we apply two regularization strategies: (1)~\\emph{confidence anchoring}, which fixes labels for conf${\\geq}$2 windows and restricts EM updates to conf${=}1$ windows; and (2)~a prior strength of 5000 on transition parameters.\n220\tThese constraints keep the model close to the majority vote baseline while allowing it to resolve temporally ambiguous labels.\n221\t\n222\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n223\t\\subsection{Label Quality: Extended Analysis}\n224\t\\label{sec:app_label_quality}\n225\t\n226\tWith corrected labels, the 3-LF probe improves from zero-shot 0.598 to fine-tuned 0.976 (previously 0.7512 under corrupted labels).\n227\tThe improvement is most dramatic at low data fractions: 5\\% fine-tuning reaches 0.850, exceeding the old 50\\% result (0.69) by 16.0\\,pp.\n228\tThe probe converges rapidly: at 50\\% OH data it reaches 0.968, essentially matching structural GB (0.967).\n229\t\n230\tThe 29.4\\,pp drop upon removing cmd\\_rep is not caused by label accuracy improvement.\n231\tAfter correction, cmd\\_rep fires on only 5.39\\% of OH windows (down from the previously reported 92.2\\% under corrupted embeddings) with 97.91\\% precision---a high-precision, low-recall signal.\n232\tOn the 4,070 windows where both 2-LF and 3-LF labels are defined, the two configurations agree on 100\\% of labels: cmd\\_rep does not change any label where the other two LFs already vote.\n233\tIts contribution is purely \\emph{coverage}: including cmd\\_rep expands the training set from ${\\sim}$4K to ${\\sim}$43K windows (a ${\\sim}$10$\\times$ increase).\n234\tThe 2-LF model's low F1 reflects data volume collapse---too few training examples to learn the stagnation distribution---rather than label corruption.\n235\t\n236\tThese results reframe how labeling functions contribute to weak supervision pipelines.\n237\tA high-precision, low-recall LF like cmd\\_rep may appear redundant when its labels are a subset of the majority vote, yet removing it causes training data to collapse by an order of magnitude.\n238\tThe core contribution of such LFs is coverage provision, not label refinement.\n239\t\n240\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n241\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Model Results}\n242\t\\label{sec:app_tlm_results}\n243\t\n244\t\\begin{table}[h]\n245\t\\centering\n246\t\\small\n247\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n248\t\\toprule\n249\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{MV Agr.} & \\textbf{Conf${\\geq}$2 F1} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs MV} & \\textbf{Probe F1$^\\ddagger$} \\\\\n250\t\\midrule\n251\tMV (baseline) & 1.000 & 0.8946$^\\ast$ & --- & 0.8468 \\\\\n252\t\\midrule\n253\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\emph{Temporal}} \\\\\n254\tTWV ($\\sigma{=}2$) & 0.885 & \\textbf{0.9003} & $+$0.6\\,pp & 0.8211 \\\\\n255\tLag-HMM & 0.820 & 0.8936 & $-$0.1\\,pp & --- \\\\\n256\tHMM & 0.685 & 0.7502 & $-$14.4\\,pp & 0.6564 \\\\\n257\t\\midrule\n258\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\emph{Generative (i.i.d.)}} \\\\\n259\tFlyingSquid & 0.820 & 0.7820 & $-$11.3\\,pp & --- \\\\\n260\tSnorkel MeTaL & 0.423 & 0.2580 & $-$63.6\\,pp & --- \\\\\n261\t\\bottomrule\n262\t\\end{tabular}\n263\t\\caption{Label model comparison. Conf${\\geq}$2 F1: each model's labels evaluated against MV labels on conf${\\geq}$2 windows. $^\\ast$MV conf${\\geq}$2 F1 (0.8946) reflects downstream probe performance when trained on MV labels, not self-agreement (which would be 1.0). $^\\ddagger$Downstream probe trained on each method's full labels, evaluated on the SWE-Agent test set ($N{=}8{,}101$) against MV labels. ``---'' = not evaluated downstream. No model consistently improves over MV.}\n264\t\\label{tab:temporal_models}\n265\t\\end{table}\n266\t\n267\t\\paragraph{Temporal models.}\n268\tTWV achieves the highest conf${\\geq}$2 F1 (0.900, $+$0.6\\,pp over MV), but this gain does not propagate downstream: a probe trained on TWV labels scores 0.821 vs.\\ 0.847 for MV-trained ($-$2.57\\,pp).\n269\tTWV flips 14.1\\% of conf${=}$1 labels, introducing noise that hurts probe training.\n270\tThe Gaussian kernel bandwidth $\\sigma$ is critical: $\\sigma{=}2$ is optimal; $\\sigma{=}1$ drops to 0.811 and $\\sigma{=}5$ to 0.808, making TWV impractical as a drop-in MV replacement.\n271\tHMM overpredicts stagnation (29,176 vs.\\ 25,633 progress windows), degrading conf${\\geq}$2 F1 by 14.4\\,pp and downstream performance to 0.656.\n272\tLag-HMM closely tracks MV ($-$0.1\\,pp) despite explicitly modeling temporal offsets.\n273\t\n274\t\\paragraph{Generative (i.i.d.) models.}\n275\tFlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, a closed-form triplet-based model, drops 11.3\\,pp below MV.\n276\tOn the full dataset, agreement with MV is 82\\%; all divergent labels flip progress$\\to$stagnation, introducing systematic stagnation bias.\n277\tSnorkel MeTaL \\citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal} fails catastrophically ($-$63.6\\,pp), with only 42.3\\% MV agreement.\n278\tBoth models assume conditional independence among LFs---an assumption violated by the shared behavioral signals underlying our three LFs---and lack the sequential structure to recover from this misspecification.\n279\t\n280\t\\paragraph{Root cause: insufficient inter-LF conflict.}\n281\tThree binary LFs produce at most $2^3{=}8$ voting patterns; conf${\\geq}$2 windows dominate the training set, leaving insufficient ambiguity for any aggregation method to exploit.\n282\tIn the current 3-LF, low-conflict regime (conf${\\geq}$2 agreement rate 95.1\\%), temporal alignment gains are offset by noise introduction.\n283\t\n284\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n285\t\\subsection{Label Model Failure Analysis}\n286\t\\label{sec:app_why_tlm}\n287\t\n288\tDespite clear temporal structure in LF activations (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}), no label model---temporal or generative---improves downstream detection over majority vote.\n289\tThe failure spans five models with distinct inductive biases: TWV and Lag-HMM model temporal dynamics, HMM captures latent state transitions, while FlyingSquid and Snorkel MeTaL assume conditional independence.\n290\t\n291\tThe failure modes differ by model class.\n292\tTemporal models either introduce noise (TWV: $-$2.57\\,pp downstream despite $+$0.6\\,pp self-evaluated) or overpredict stagnation (HMM: $-$14.4\\,pp).\n293\tGenerative models fare worse: FlyingSquid ($-$11.3\\,pp) introduces systematic stagnation bias, and Snorkel MeTaL collapses catastrophically ($-$63.6\\,pp, 42.3\\% MV agreement).\n294\tBoth assume conditional independence among LFs---violated by the shared behavioral signals underlying our three functions---and lack sequential structure to recover from this misspecification.\n295\t\n296\tThe bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation sophistication.\n297\tThree binary LFs represent a realistic operating point for domain-specific agent monitoring, where each LF requires expert knowledge of agent failure modes.\n298\tOur finding that temporal label models fail in this low-LF regime has direct implications for practitioners who cannot afford to design ${>}$10 labeling functions: majority vote is not merely a convenient baseline but the recommended aggregation strategy.\n299\tScaling to a richer LF set with higher disagreement rates would be needed to create the ambiguity where advanced aggregation could add value \\citep{ratner2019snorkel}.\n300\t\n301\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n302\t\\subsection{Error Analysis}\n303\t\\label{sec:app_error_analysis}\n304\t\n305\t\\paragraph{In-domain errors.}\n306\tWe analyze errors of the ProgressProbe (GRU) on the SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set under the stagnation-positive convention (positive = stagnation throughout).\n307\tThe model misclassifies 131 of 1,824 windows (7.2\\% error rate): 85 false negatives (predicted progress, actual stagnation; 64.9\\% of errors) and 46 false positives (predicted stagnation, actual progress; 35.1\\%).\n308\t\n309\tThe recall bottleneck is the dominant failure mode: $P_\\text{stag}{=}0.897$ vs.\\ $R_\\text{stag}{=}0.825$.\n310\tMissed stagnation windows (FN) are not borderline cases---the model assigns them a mean stagnation probability of only 0.078 (i.e., high progress confidence of 0.922), with 73\\% receiving $>$0.9 progress probability.\n311\tThese windows feature agents performing diverse edits and varied commands that mimic progress patterns while making no effective headway, constituting a systematic blind spot.\n312\tFalse positives arise when agents repeat specific command patterns during genuine progress.\n313\t\n314\tLF conflict is a strong error predictor: 39.7\\% of error windows have at least one LF disagreeing, compared to a 31\\% base rate.\n315\tThe cmd\\_rep LF contributes the highest per-LF error rate (12.75\\%).\n316\tErrors concentrate in trajectory midpoints (10.2\\% error rate vs.\\ 6.5\\% at endpoints), where the boundary between exploration and stagnation is most ambiguous.\n317\tError mass is also non-uniform across trajectories: the two highest-error trajectories account for 18.3\\% of all misclassifications.\n318\t\n319\t\\paragraph{Cross-agent error patterns.}\n320\tWe extend error analysis to cross-coding-agent transfer, evaluating the SWE-trained probe and structural GB on the full OH conf${\\geq}$2 set (43,376 windows).\n321\tThe probe misclassifies 19.9\\% of windows, while structural GB errs on only 0.51\\% (223 windows)---a nearly 40$\\times$ gap that quantifies the distributional shift penalty for learned embeddings.\n322\tOf the probe's errors, 80.5\\% are false positives (predicting stagnation on progress windows), indicating that the probe systematically misreads OH action patterns as stagnation signals learned from SWE-Agent.\n323\t\n324\tThe two models' errors are nearly disjoint: only 2.2\\% of probe errors overlap with structural errors (192 of 8,643 probe-error windows).\n325\tStructural features, being agent-agnostic by construction, are immune to the embedding distribution shift that drives probe failures.\n326\t\n327\tThe probe's cross-coding-agent errors are not borderline: 62\\% occur at high confidence ($>$0.9 or $<$0.1), ruling out threshold tuning as a remedy.\n328\tThe embedding space encodes SWE-Agent--specific stagnation patterns that confidently misfire on OH distributions, explaining why even 5\\% target fine-tuning (reaching 0.850 macro-F1) dramatically outperforms zero-shot transfer.\n329\t\n330\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n331\t\\subsection{Outcome Backpropagation as Distant Supervision}\n332\t\\label{sec:app_ob_analysis}\n333\t\n334\tOutcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$) is a privileged signal that uses task outcome, unavailable at inference time.\n335\tA 3-seed split-isolated holdout removing $\\lambda_3$ yields macro-F1 of $0.770{\\pm}0.022$, a 7.7\\,pp drop from the 3-LF baseline of 0.847.\n336\t$\\lambda_3$ contributes 33.5\\% of conf${\\geq}$2 labels (5,158 of 15,401 windows).\n337\t\n338\tHowever, $\\lambda_3$ functions as a coverage expander rather than an independent corrector: across 43,402 windows where both 2-LF and 3-LF configurations produce labels, their agreement is 100\\%.\n339\t$\\lambda_3$ never flips a majority vote---it only promotes conf${=}1$ windows to conf${=}2$ by adding a third agreeing vote.\n340\tThis suggests that additional real-time LFs targeting the same coverage gaps could replace the privileged outcome signal without sacrificing label quality.\n341\t\n342\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n343\t\\subsection{Labeling Function Temporal Dynamics}\n344\t\\label{sec:app_lf_temporal}\n345\t\n346\t\\begin{table}[h]\n347\t\\centering\n348\t\\small\n349\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n350\t\\toprule\n351\t\\textbf{LF} & \\textbf{Lag} & \\textbf{Lead\\,\\%} & \\textbf{Conc.\\,\\%} & \\textbf{Lag\\,\\%} \\\\\n352\t\\midrule\n353\tcmd\\_rep      & $-1.87$ & 36.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n354\tfile\\_diff    & $-1.20$ & 17.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n355\toutcome\\_bp   & $+6.70$ & ---  & --- & 48.4 \\\\\n356\t\\bottomrule\n357\t\\end{tabular}\n358\t\\caption{Temporal roles of labeling functions on SWE-Agent. Lag = mean steps relative to stagnation onset (negative = leading). Kruskal-Wallis $H{=}274.3$, $p{=}2.74{\\times}10^{-60}$; all pairwise comparisons significant after Bonferroni correction.}\n359\t\\label{tab:lf_temporal}\n360\t\\end{table}\n361\t\n362\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n363\t\\centering\n364\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_lf_temporal.pdf}\n365\t\\caption{Temporal roles of labeling functions relative to stagnation onset on SWE-Agent. cmd\\_rep leads ($-1.87$ steps), file\\_diff is near-concurrent ($-1.20$), outcome\\_bp lags ($+6.70$). Inset: OH reversal where cmd\\_rep becomes lagging.}\n366\t\\label{fig:lf_temporal}\n367\t\\end{figure}\n368\t\n369\tAll pairwise differences are significant (Bonferroni-corrected: cmd\\_rep vs.\\ file\\_diff $p{=}1.44{\\times}10^{-26}$, cmd\\_rep vs.\\ outcome\\_bp $p{=}6.67{\\times}10^{-39}$, file\\_diff vs.\\ outcome\\_bp $p{=}3.47{\\times}10^{-47}$).\n370\t\n371\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n372\t\\subsection{Gold Set Validation Protocol}\n373\t\\label{sec:app_gold_set}\n374\t\n375\tWe sample 650 windows stratified along two axes---\\emph{confidence} (balanced across conf${=}1$, conf${=}2$, and conf${=}3$ strata) and \\emph{trajectory position} (early, middle, and late thirds)---for non-circular evaluation.\n376\tOf these, 512 received at least one annotation.\n377\t\n378\tThree CS graduate students independently label each window as stagnation or progress using an operational definition: \\emph{a window is stagnation if the agent's actions within it do not make meaningful progress toward solving the task, regardless of whether the actions appear diverse or purposeful}.\n379\tAnnotators receive the $k{=}5$ action descriptions with full command outputs and file diffs, but \\emph{not} the task outcome (resolved/unresolved), preventing information leakage from $\\lambda_3$.\n380\t\n381\t\\paragraph{Evaluation sets.}\n382\tWe define three evaluation sets from the 512 annotated windows:\n383\tSet~A ($N{=}52$): windows labeled by all three annotators (used for IAA);\n384\tSet~B ($N{=}505$): windows where ${\\geq}$2 annotators agree on the gold label (primary evaluation);\n385\tSet~C ($N{=}512$): all windows with ${\\geq}$1 annotation.\n386\tTable~\\ref{tab:gold_batch1} reports detection performance on Set~B.\n387\t\n388\t\\paragraph{Inter-annotator agreement.}\n389\t\\label{sec:app_iaa}\n390\tOn the 52 three-annotator windows (Set~A), Fleiss' $\\kappa{=}0.39$ (fair agreement); pairwise Cohen's $\\kappa$ ranges from 0.33 to 0.49.\n391\tThe moderate agreement reflects the inherent subjectivity of stagnation boundaries: an agent exploring alternative strategies may appear stuck or productive depending on annotator interpretation, particularly when actions are semantically diverse but ultimately unproductive.\n392\tThis subjectivity motivates the weak supervision approach: rather than relying on expensive and inherently noisy human labels, the system derives silver labels from complementary heuristic signals.\n393\t\n394\t\\paragraph{Gold detection results.}\n395\tTable~\\ref{tab:gold_batch1} reports model performance against Set~B gold labels.\n396\t\n397\t\\begin{table}[h]\n398\t\\centering\n399\t\\small\n400\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n401\t\\toprule\n402\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Scope} & $N$ & \\textbf{Macro-F1} \\\\\n403\t\\midrule\n404\tStructural GB   & SWE-only & ${\\sim}$335 & \\textbf{0.877} \\\\\n405\tTWV Probe       & SWE-only & ${\\sim}$335 & 0.854 \\\\\n406\t\\midrule\n407\tStructural GB   & All      & 505 & \\textbf{0.839} \\\\\n408\tStructural RF   & All      & 505 & \\underline{0.811} \\\\\n409\tMV Labels       & All      & 505 & 0.798 \\\\\n410\tGRU Probe       & SWE-only & ${\\sim}$335 & 0.790 \\\\\n411\t\\bottomrule\n412\t\\end{tabular}\n413\t\\caption{Gold set detection results (Set~B, $N{=}505$, ${\\geq}$2-annotator agreement; 3 CS graduate students, Fleiss' $\\kappa{=}0.39$). GB outperforms MV by 4.2\\,pp (paired bootstrap $p{<}0.001$, $B{=}10{,}000$). Probe models are evaluated on the SWE-only subset ($N{\\approx}335$) due to missing OH embeddings; scope-matched, GB (0.877) exceeds TWV Probe (0.854). \\textbf{Bold} = best within scope, \\underline{underline} = second.}\n414\t\\label{tab:gold_batch1}\n415\t\\end{table}\n416\t\n417\tGB vs.\\ MV: $+$4.2\\,pp ($p{<}0.001$); GB vs.\\ RF: $+$2.9\\,pp ($p{<}0.001$); GB vs.\\ GRU Probe: $+$5.0\\,pp on the full set (paired bootstrap on shared SWE-only subset: $+$8.8\\,pp, $p{<}0.001$).\n418\tOn the SWE-only subset, GB (0.877) exceeds TWV Probe (0.854) by 2.3\\,pp ($p{=}0.056$, not significant at $\\alpha{=}0.05$); the TWV advantage in the overall ranking reflects scope mismatch, not superior detection.\n419\tSet~A ($N{=}52$) and Set~C ($N{=}512$) yield consistent rankings (GB $>$ MV in all sets).\n420\t\n421\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n422\t\\subsection{Feature Overlap Ablation Table}\n423\t\\label{sec:app_feature_overlap}\n424\t\n425\t\\begin{table}[h]\n426\t\\centering\n427\t\\small\n428\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n429\t\\toprule\n430\t\\textbf{Feature Set} & \\textbf{\\#Feat} & \\textbf{In-Dom.} & \\textbf{Cross-Ag.} \\\\\n431\t\\midrule\n432\tFull (all 30) & 30 & 0.9951 & 0.9674 \\\\\n433\tLF-overlap only & 11 & 0.9937 & \\textbf{0.9770} \\\\\n434\tResidual only & 19 & 0.9515 & 0.5656 \\\\\n435\t\\midrule\n436\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\emph{Leave-one-group-out (cross-coding-agent)}} \\\\\n437\t$-$ cmd\\_rep ($\\lambda_1$) & 25 & --- & 0.5620 \\\\\n438\t$-$ file\\_diff ($\\lambda_2$) & 25 & --- & 0.6590 \\\\\n439\t$-$ outcome\\_bp ($\\lambda_3$) & 29 & --- & 0.9660 \\\\\n440\t\\bottomrule\n441\t\\end{tabular}\n442\t\\caption{Feature overlap ablation (GB). In-Dom.\\ = SWE-Agent in-domain macro-F1; Cross-Ag.\\ = SWE-Agent $\\to$ OpenHands zero-shot. Top: on all OH windows, LF-overlap features alone exceed the full model cross-coding-agent ($+$1.0\\,pp; on conf${\\geq}$2, direction reverses by $-$0.71\\,pp); residual features collapse. Bottom: removing each LF-group individually; cmd\\_rep features account for the largest transfer drop ($-$40.6\\,pp).}\n443\t\\label{tab:feature_overlap}\n444\t\\end{table}\n445\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jd5fi31Jun3QQkhFFUSeJ9","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"ddf0c83c-3d61-4080-a312-0964d72b4b11","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:30:57.320Z"}
