{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0168ubt2XgWeu7FkkF782Eac","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:16:\\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document coding agent degradation; \\citet{chen2025monitoring} show sequence-level context is necessary; \\citet{kale2025weaktostrong} show weak monitors can oversee stronger agents; and \\citet{cemri2025multiagentfail,zhu2025agentfail} catalog systematic failure modes.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:147:Three CS graduate students annotate a stratified gold set; 512 windows received at least one annotation, with Set~B ($N{=}505$, ${\\geq}$2 annotators agree) as the primary evaluation (protocol and IAA in Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_gold_set}).\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:19:Given an agent trajectory $T = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n)$ where each $a_t$ is an action (e.g., a shell command, file edit, or API call), we partition it into overlapping sliding windows $w_i = (a_i, \\ldots, a_{i+k-1})$ with window size $k{=}5$ and stride 1.\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:24:Compared to trajectory-level labeling, windows provide the granularity needed to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a single task attempt, enabling downstream interventions such as early termination or strategy switching.\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:51:Uses the final task outcome as distant supervision: unresolved tasks' last windows receive stagnation votes; resolved tasks receive progress votes; all others abstain.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:310:Missed stagnation windows (FN) are not borderline cases---the model assigns them a mean stagnation probability of only 0.078 (i.e., high progress confidence of 0.922), with 73\\% receiving $>$0.9 progress probability.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:376:Of these, 512 received at least one annotation.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:379:Annotators receive the $k{=}5$ action descriptions with full command outputs and file diffs, but \\emph{not} the task outcome (resolved/unresolved), preventing information leakage from $\\lambda_3$.\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:23:Our window-level formulation ($k{=}5$ consecutive actions, stride 1) captures short-range temporal patterns---such as repeated failures followed by strategy changes---that step-level approaches cannot express, while providing the granularity to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a trajectory, enabling targeted interventions."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011px2qzXanuBtKfZzqbMU3j","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"19353d23-c3a3-4b67-9588-84851ce86303","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:31:12.262Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YMusZLjEcTBZdNz5B7cbKh","type":"tool_result","content":"149\tProbe models are evaluated on the SWE-only subset ($N{\\approx}335$) due to missing OH embeddings; cross-method comparisons should account for differing $N$ (Table~\\ref{tab:gold_batch1})."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jd5fi31Jun3QQkhFFUSeJ9","session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"b63a5ac4-219b-439c-bc37-83da9f8293cc","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:31:12.284Z"}
