{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011px2qzXanuBtKfZzqbMU3j","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of both what the paper claims and what is actually written. The summary provided to me references many tables (4-13) and analyses (SHAP, calibration, ECE, power analysis, stride sensitivity, early termination, stagnation tail) that are NOT present in the actual .tex files. The paper as written has 7 tables in the appendix and 2 in the main text, plus the cmd_rep ablation table. These additional analyses exist as planned experiments (experiment_plan.yaml) and as standalone Python scripts, but are not incorporated into the paper text.\n\nLet me now produce the review based on the actual paper content.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n**Paper:** \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper studies whether structural (hand-crafted) features suffice for detecting stagnation in LLM coding agent trajectories, using a weak supervision pipeline with three labeling functions over 205K windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands. The central finding is a deployment-regime trade-off: structural features achieve 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1 with zero target data, outperforming neural probes (0.598) and LLM judges (0.799), but fine-tuned probes surpass structural features on all-confidence windows (0.976 vs. 0.967). The paper also reports that five temporal label models fail to beat majority vote, and that label quality (not model capacity) is the dominant transfer bottleneck.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Honest acknowledgment of circularity.** The paper explicitly flags that the 0.995 in-domain structural F1 reflects LF reconstruction, not ground-truth accuracy, and provides three circularity-breaking controls: cross-agent transfer, instance isolation (Table in appendix showing <0.1pp change), and gold validation. This is commendable self-awareness that is rare in empirical ML papers.\n\n- **Comprehensive ablation battery.** The paper covers encoder ablation (3 encoders), window size ablation, data scaling curves, architecture comparison (GRU vs. Transformer), multi-seed stability, and feature overlap decomposition (11 LF-overlapping vs. 19 residual features). The leave-one-group-out ablation localizing cross-agent signal to cmd_rep features (-40.6pp) and file_diff features (-30.8pp) is particularly informative.\n\n- **Statistical testing throughout.** Paired bootstrap CIs are reported for key comparisons (e.g., GB vs. probe: CI [1.11, 2.23]pp on conf>=2), McNemar tests for LF ablation (chi^2=1551), Kruskal-Wallis with Bonferroni correction for temporal dynamics. The paper does not rely on point estimates alone.\n\n- **Meaningful negative result on temporal label models.** The systematic evaluation of five label models (TWV, HMM, Lag-HMM, FlyingSquid, Snorkel MeTaL) with a clear diagnosis of the failure mode (insufficient inter-LF conflict from only 3 binary LFs) is a genuine contribution to the weak supervision literature.\n\n- **Label quality audit as a finding.** The discovery that a version mismatch in OH embeddings shifted stagnation rate from 6.56% to 21.4% and dominated transfer performance is a practically important observation for weak supervision practitioners.\n\n- **Feature overlap ablation is well-designed.** The finding that 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 (exceeding the full model by 1.0pp on all windows) while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566 is a clean decomposition. The reversal on conf>=2 (where residual features help) adds nuance.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The headline 0.967 cross-agent F1 is still evaluated against silver labels.** The paper's primary performance claim---that structural features achieve 0.967 on OpenHands---is evaluated against majority-vote silver labels on the target domain. Since the feature overlap ablation shows that LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 (even exceeding the full model), this score predominantly measures how well features recover the LF voting pattern on a new agent, not how well they detect actual stagnation. The gold validation (0.839 on N=505) is much more informative but is relegated to a subordinate role. The paper should foreground the gold-set F1 as the primary result and contextualize the silver-label scores as LF-agreement metrics.\n\n- **Gold set is severely underpowered and has low inter-annotator agreement.** Fleiss' kappa=0.39 (fair agreement) on only N=52 three-annotator windows is marginal for establishing a reliable ground truth. The primary evaluation set (Set B, N=505) uses majority of >=2 annotators, but with kappa=0.39, the gold labels themselves have substantial noise. GB's 0.839 gold F1 should be interpreted against this noisy ceiling. The paper does not report a formal power analysis for the gold set; the summary mentions one (Table 10) but this is not present in the manuscript text. Without knowing what effect sizes N=505 can detect, it is impossible to evaluate whether the 4.2pp GB-vs-MV advantage is meaningfully different from noise at finer granularity.\n\n- **Stride-1 windowing creates massive autocorrelation, inflating effective N.** With stride=1 and window size k=5, consecutive windows overlap by 4/5 actions. The 54,809 SWE-Agent windows and 150,652 OH windows have far fewer independent observations. All reported sample sizes, confidence intervals, and p-values are computed as if windows are independent. The paper acknowledges this as a limitation but does not quantify the effective sample size or adjust statistical tests. This is a methodological concern that undermines the precision of all reported confidence intervals. A stride sensitivity analysis is mentioned in the experiment plan but does not appear in the paper.\n\n- **Outcome backpropagation LF ($\\lambda_3$) uses information unavailable at inference time.** The paper acknowledges this (Limitations section) but underplays its severity. lambda_3 contributes 33.5% of conf>=2 labels. The 2-LF configuration (without lambda_3) is mentioned in the cmd_rep ablation context, but the paper does not clearly report what happens to the cross-agent transfer score when lambda_3 is removed from the silver labeling pipeline entirely. The leave-one-group-out feature ablation shows removing outcome_bp features costs only 0.2pp, but this tests feature removal, not label removal---the labels used for training still include lambda_3 votes. A deployment-ready pipeline must function without outcome information; the paper does not convincingly demonstrate this.\n\n- **Several analyses mentioned in the paper summary are missing from the manuscript.** The review context lists SHAP feature importance (Table 9), calibration/ECE analysis (Table 8), gold stratified results (Table 4), conf=1 error decomposition (Table 5), human ceiling estimation (Table 6), power analysis (Table 10), stride sensitivity (Table 11), early termination simulation (Table 12), and stagnation tail statistics (Table 13). None of these appear in the actual .tex files. These are critical analyses: calibration is essential for any deployment claim, power analysis is needed to interpret the gold set, and early termination is the practical motivation. Their absence significantly weakens the paper.\n\n- **The claim of \"Pareto-efficient deployment\" is not substantiated.** The abstract and introduction claim structural features offer Pareto-efficient deployment (<5ms, $0 cost), but no Pareto curve is presented. The comparison to LLM judges (Table in appendix) is on a different evaluation set (SWE-Agent in-domain) than the structural cross-agent score (OH), making the 16.8pp gap a cross-context comparison. The LLM evaluation is also limited: Claude Sonnet 4.6 and GPT-5.5 only, with GPT-5.5 having a 22.4% parse failure rate that artificially depresses its score.\n\n- **Cross-agent transfer tests exactly two agents in the same domain.** SWE-Agent and OpenHands are both coding agents running on SWE-bench. Despite the paper's careful language (\"cross-coding-agent\"), the generalization claim is based on a single transfer pair. The 284 shared SWE-bench task instances (8.3% of OH) are controlled via instance isolation, which is good, but both agents operate in the same task distribution. Transfer to a web navigation agent or a non-coding domain remains untested.\n\n- **Feature overlap ablation reveals the structural model is essentially an LF reconstructor.** On all OH windows, LF-overlapping features alone (11 features) outperform the full model (0.977 vs. 0.967). This means the 19 residual features actually hurt cross-agent performance. The paper interprets this as \"the features that generalize encode cross-agent stagnation signals,\" but the simpler interpretation is that the structural model's cross-agent success comes entirely from reconstructing the LF voting pattern, which happens to partially generalize across these two coding agents. The 0.977 number for LF-overlap-only is the most concerning finding in the paper and receives insufficient critical discussion.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. What is the cross-agent transfer performance of the structural GB model when trained on silver labels produced without $\\lambda_3$ (outcome backpropagation)? The 2-LF ablation in Table 3 shows the probe's performance degrades, but the structural model's performance under 2-LF labels is not reported for the deployment-relevant configuration.\n\n2. The gold set evaluation (GB 0.839, MV 0.798) is the paper's strongest non-circular evidence. What is the statistical power of this comparison? With N=505 and the observed effect size, what is the minimum detectable difference at 80% power?\n\n3. The feature overlap ablation shows LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent---exceeding the full model. How should readers interpret the \"structural features detect stagnation\" claim when 100% of cross-agent generalization (and more) comes from features that overlap with the labeling functions?\n\n4. How do the bootstrap confidence intervals change when adjusting for autocorrelation from stride-1 windowing? Even a simple effective-N correction using the autocorrelation function of consecutive window labels would help calibrate the reported CIs.\n\n5. The LLM judge evaluation reports GPT-5.5 with a 22.4% parse failure rate (originally 9.4% in the summary). Were failed parses treated as errors or excluded? This choice significantly affects the reported macro-F1.\n\n6. Why are the calibration, SHAP, power analysis, stride sensitivity, early termination, and stagnation tail analyses---all listed in the experiment plan and with scripts present in the repository---not included in the manuscript?\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract claims \"0.967 vs. best LLM 0.799\" as a 16.8pp gap, but these are evaluated on different datasets (OH transfer vs. SWE-Agent in-domain for the LLM). This cross-context comparison should be flagged more prominently.\n- Table 1 footnote $^a$ notes the probe is evaluated on N=6,122 while structural models on N=150,652. This scope mismatch makes direct comparison in the same table misleading.\n- The paper title asks \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?\" but the answer the paper provides is closer to \"Structural features reconstruct LF votes well, and LF votes partially transfer across agents.\" The title oversells the finding.\n- Some references have placeholder-style formatting (e.g., \"and others\" instead of proper author lists in the .bib file).\n- The claim that cmd_rep's value is \"purely coverage\" (100% agreement on overlapping windows) is somewhat misleading---it means cmd_rep never disagrees with the 2-LF consensus, but cmd_rep's high-precision votes on previously-unlabeled windows do add real information (otherwise random coverage expansion would also help).\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper tackles a relevant problem (detecting stagnation in LLM agents) and demonstrates commendable honesty about its circularity limitations. The ablation battery is extensive by the standards of empirical ML papers, and the negative result on temporal label models is a genuine contribution.\n\nHowever, several methodological concerns prevent acceptance at a top venue:\n\n(a) The headline result (0.967 cross-agent F1) is evaluated against silver labels that the structural features are designed to reconstruct. The feature overlap ablation confirms this: LF-overlapping features alone exceed the full model cross-agent. The paper acknowledges this but still foregrounds the silver-label metrics.\n\n(b) The gold validation---the paper's most credible evidence---is underpowered (N=505, kappa=0.39) and reveals a modest advantage (0.839 vs. 0.798) that the paper does not formally power-analyze. Several planned analyses (calibration, power analysis, stride sensitivity, SHAP, early termination simulation) that would strengthen the claims are absent from the manuscript despite having scripts in the repository.\n\n(c) The scope is narrow (2 coding agents on the same benchmark), the outcome-dependent LF creates a deployment gap that is not resolved, and the stride-1 autocorrelation is not quantified.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (1) foregrounding gold-set results as primary, (2) including the missing analyses (especially calibration, power, stride sensitivity), (3) demonstrating the pipeline works without $\\lambda_3$, and (4) being more explicit that the structural model's cross-agent success is primarily LF reconstruction that happens to partially generalize."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: abdcbed789e1d57b9 (use SendMessage with to: 'abdcbed789e1d57b9' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 63577\ntool_uses: 24\nduration_ms: 155349</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4efa5a2a-e3e9-4e76-992e-c9082babf060","uuid":"8e907e60-0430-4610-b9a2-e2a0b79d8e45","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:32:47.912Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Score on a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept), targeting ~25% acceptance rate.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Detecting Stagnation in LLM Agent Trajectories: An Empirical Analysis of Structural Features, Label Quality, and Deployment Trade-offs\"\n\nThe paper studies semantic stagnation detection in LLM agent trajectories (SWE-Agent 543 traj, OpenHands 3,409 traj) using weak supervision with 3 labeling functions (LFs: cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_bp). It compares structural classifiers (GB/RF on 30 handcrafted features), neural probes (Qwen3-0.6B, E5-large-v2, BGE-M3), and LLM judges (Claude variants, GPT-5.5).\n\n**4 Main Claims:**\n1. Label quality dominates transfer: fixing version-induced label bias on OpenHands yields +28pp improvement in probe fine-tuning\n2. Structural features offer Pareto-efficient deployment: 0.967 F1, <5ms, $0 cost vs best LLM 0.872 at $47/2K windows\n3. Temporal label models (TWV, HMM-TLM, Lag-aware HMM) all fail to beat majority vote — negative result\n4. Early termination shows limited potential: best saves 13.1% compute at 83.4% task success preservation\n\n**Gold validation:** 505 windows, 3 annotators, Fleiss κ=0.39, pairwise agreement ~80%. GB 0.839 vs MV 0.798 overall (p<0.001), but advantage concentrates on conf≥2 (Δ=+4.0pp, p=0.003); conf=1 inconclusive (Δ=−2.0pp, p=0.31). Human ceiling ~0.82 F1.\n\n**Key Tables:**\n- Table 1: Cross-agent transfer. Structural GB 0.967, Claude fs-CoT 0.872 (-9.5pp), BGE-M3 probe 0.780 (-18.7pp), Qwen probe 0.598 (-36.9pp)\n- Table 2: Encoder comparison. Gap narrows from 36.9pp (Qwen) to 18.7pp (BGE-M3)\n- Table 3: 2-LF coverage. 66.5% on SWE-Agent but only 9.3% conf≥2 on OpenHands\n- Table 4: Gold stratified. conf=1 N=282 GB F1=0.66, MV Acc=0.71; conf≥2 N=223 GB F1=0.87\n- Table 5: Error decomposition on conf=1. GB-correct-MV-wrong=43, GB-wrong-MV-correct=49\n- Table 6: Human ceiling. κ=0.39, ceiling ~0.82, GB 0.839 (+1.9pp above ceiling)\n- Table 7: LLM judges on OH (N=2000). GPT-5.5 has 9.4% parse failure rate\n- Table 8: Calibration. GB gold ECE_raw=0.118, ECE_temp=0.058. Mid-range overconfident\n- Table 9: SHAP. Top 2 features (repeated_cmd_ratio, unique_file_edits) are LF-overlapping, 72.3% importance from LF-overlapping features\n- Table 10: Power analysis. conf=1 power=0.12, GB vs RF power=0.41\n- Table 11: Stride sensitivity. stride=5 CI width ~2x stride=1\n- Table 12: Early termination sweep. k=3 saves 13.1%, 83.4% task success\n- Table 13: Stagnation tail stats. 38.2% tailed, 18% waste steps, 15.9% waste tokens\n\n**Discussion:** Early termination is \"preliminary evidence.\" Circularity acknowledged: 72.3% SHAP from LF-overlapping features. Structural model ≈ 70% LF reconstruction + 30% residual.\n\n**Limitations (6 items):** Gold set power, scope (2 coding agents), 2-LF coverage collapse, encoder ceiling unknown, stride-1 autocorrelation, early termination simulation-only, calibration issues.\n\n---\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiments checklist)\n\nThe following experiments were planned. Check which are covered and which are missing:\n1. Compute Waste Audit → Table 13, §5.1\n2. Early Termination Simulation → Table 12, §5.1\n3. Cost-Effectiveness Pareto → §5.1 (AUC numbers mentioned)\n4. LLM-as-Judge on OH → Table 7, §4.7\n5. Conf=1 Error Decomposition → Table 5, §4.5\n6. BGE-M3 Probe → Table 2, §4.2\n7. E5-large Probe → Table 2, §4.2\n8. 2-LF Coverage → Table 3, §4.4\n9. Calibration → Table 8, §4.8\n10. Gold Power Analysis → Table 10, §4.11\n11. Human Ceiling → Table 6, §4.6\n12. Stride Sensitivity → Table 11, §4.12\n13. SHAP Feature Importance → Table 9, §4.9\n14. Trajectory Visualization → NOT in paper (missing)\n\n---\n\n## Your Review (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: experimental methodology, statistical rigor, comprehensiveness of ablations, honesty of reporting\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence)\nFocus on: methodological gaps, statistical concerns, missing experiments, circular evaluation, experimental design flaws\n\n### Questions for Authors (numbered)\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n### Preliminary Score (1-6) with justification\n\nBe rigorous. A top venue at 25% acceptance means scores of 3 (Weak Reject) or below are common for papers with significant methodological concerns. Pay close attention to:\n- The circularity problem (structural model trained on LF-derived labels, evaluated on LF-derived silver labels giving 0.995 F1)\n- The gold set's statistical power limitations\n- Whether the 0.967 F1 claim is meaningful given it's evaluated on silver labels\n- The autocorrelation issue with stride-1 windows\n- Whether the paper's claims are properly calibrated to its evidence\n","agentId":"abdcbed789e1d57b9","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of both what the paper claims and what is actually written. The summary provided to me references many tables (4-13) and analyses (SHAP, calibration, ECE, power analysis, stride sensitivity, early termination, stagnation tail) that are NOT present in the actual .tex files. The paper as written has 7 tables in the appendix and 2 in the main text, plus the cmd_rep ablation table. These additional analyses exist as planned experiments (experiment_plan.yaml) and as standalone Python scripts, but are not incorporated into the paper text.\n\nLet me now produce the review based on the actual paper content.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n**Paper:** \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural? A Systematic Empirical Study of Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper studies whether structural (hand-crafted) features suffice for detecting stagnation in LLM coding agent trajectories, using a weak supervision pipeline with three labeling functions over 205K windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands. The central finding is a deployment-regime trade-off: structural features achieve 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1 with zero target data, outperforming neural probes (0.598) and LLM judges (0.799), but fine-tuned probes surpass structural features on all-confidence windows (0.976 vs. 0.967). The paper also reports that five temporal label models fail to beat majority vote, and that label quality (not model capacity) is the dominant transfer bottleneck.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Honest acknowledgment of circularity.** The paper explicitly flags that the 0.995 in-domain structural F1 reflects LF reconstruction, not ground-truth accuracy, and provides three circularity-breaking controls: cross-agent transfer, instance isolation (Table in appendix showing <0.1pp change), and gold validation. This is commendable self-awareness that is rare in empirical ML papers.\n\n- **Comprehensive ablation battery.** The paper covers encoder ablation (3 encoders), window size ablation, data scaling curves, architecture comparison (GRU vs. Transformer), multi-seed stability, and feature overlap decomposition (11 LF-overlapping vs. 19 residual features). The leave-one-group-out ablation localizing cross-agent signal to cmd_rep features (-40.6pp) and file_diff features (-30.8pp) is particularly informative.\n\n- **Statistical testing throughout.** Paired bootstrap CIs are reported for key comparisons (e.g., GB vs. probe: CI [1.11, 2.23]pp on conf>=2), McNemar tests for LF ablation (chi^2=1551), Kruskal-Wallis with Bonferroni correction for temporal dynamics. The paper does not rely on point estimates alone.\n\n- **Meaningful negative result on temporal label models.** The systematic evaluation of five label models (TWV, HMM, Lag-HMM, FlyingSquid, Snorkel MeTaL) with a clear diagnosis of the failure mode (insufficient inter-LF conflict from only 3 binary LFs) is a genuine contribution to the weak supervision literature.\n\n- **Label quality audit as a finding.** The discovery that a version mismatch in OH embeddings shifted stagnation rate from 6.56% to 21.4% and dominated transfer performance is a practically important observation for weak supervision practitioners.\n\n- **Feature overlap ablation is well-designed.** The finding that 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 (exceeding the full model by 1.0pp on all windows) while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566 is a clean decomposition. The reversal on conf>=2 (where residual features help) adds nuance.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The headline 0.967 cross-agent F1 is still evaluated against silver labels.** The paper's primary performance claim---that structural features achieve 0.967 on OpenHands---is evaluated against majority-vote silver labels on the target domain. Since the feature overlap ablation shows that LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 (even exceeding the full model), this score predominantly measures how well features recover the LF voting pattern on a new agent, not how well they detect actual stagnation. The gold validation (0.839 on N=505) is much more informative but is relegated to a subordinate role. The paper should foreground the gold-set F1 as the primary result and contextualize the silver-label scores as LF-agreement metrics.\n\n- **Gold set is severely underpowered and has low inter-annotator agreement.** Fleiss' kappa=0.39 (fair agreement) on only N=52 three-annotator windows is marginal for establishing a reliable ground truth. The primary evaluation set (Set B, N=505) uses majority of >=2 annotators, but with kappa=0.39, the gold labels themselves have substantial noise. GB's 0.839 gold F1 should be interpreted against this noisy ceiling. The paper does not report a formal power analysis for the gold set; the summary mentions one (Table 10) but this is not present in the manuscript text. Without knowing what effect sizes N=505 can detect, it is impossible to evaluate whether the 4.2pp GB-vs-MV advantage is meaningfully different from noise at finer granularity.\n\n- **Stride-1 windowing creates massive autocorrelation, inflating effective N.** With stride=1 and window size k=5, consecutive windows overlap by 4/5 actions. The 54,809 SWE-Agent windows and 150,652 OH windows have far fewer independent observations. All reported sample sizes, confidence intervals, and p-values are computed as if windows are independent. The paper acknowledges this as a limitation but does not quantify the effective sample size or adjust statistical tests. This is a methodological concern that undermines the precision of all reported confidence intervals. A stride sensitivity analysis is mentioned in the experiment plan but does not appear in the paper.\n\n- **Outcome backpropagation LF ($\\lambda_3$) uses information unavailable at inference time.** The paper acknowledges this (Limitations section) but underplays its severity. lambda_3 contributes 33.5% of conf>=2 labels. The 2-LF configuration (without lambda_3) is mentioned in the cmd_rep ablation context, but the paper does not clearly report what happens to the cross-agent transfer score when lambda_3 is removed from the silver labeling pipeline entirely. The leave-one-group-out feature ablation shows removing outcome_bp features costs only 0.2pp, but this tests feature removal, not label removal---the labels used for training still include lambda_3 votes. A deployment-ready pipeline must function without outcome information; the paper does not convincingly demonstrate this.\n\n- **Several analyses mentioned in the paper summary are missing from the manuscript.** The review context lists SHAP feature importance (Table 9), calibration/ECE analysis (Table 8), gold stratified results (Table 4), conf=1 error decomposition (Table 5), human ceiling estimation (Table 6), power analysis (Table 10), stride sensitivity (Table 11), early termination simulation (Table 12), and stagnation tail statistics (Table 13). None of these appear in the actual .tex files. These are critical analyses: calibration is essential for any deployment claim, power analysis is needed to interpret the gold set, and early termination is the practical motivation. Their absence significantly weakens the paper.\n\n- **The claim of \"Pareto-efficient deployment\" is not substantiated.** The abstract and introduction claim structural features offer Pareto-efficient deployment (<5ms, $0 cost), but no Pareto curve is presented. The comparison to LLM judges (Table in appendix) is on a different evaluation set (SWE-Agent in-domain) than the structural cross-agent score (OH), making the 16.8pp gap a cross-context comparison. The LLM evaluation is also limited: Claude Sonnet 4.6 and GPT-5.5 only, with GPT-5.5 having a 22.4% parse failure rate that artificially depresses its score.\n\n- **Cross-agent transfer tests exactly two agents in the same domain.** SWE-Agent and OpenHands are both coding agents running on SWE-bench. Despite the paper's careful language (\"cross-coding-agent\"), the generalization claim is based on a single transfer pair. The 284 shared SWE-bench task instances (8.3% of OH) are controlled via instance isolation, which is good, but both agents operate in the same task distribution. Transfer to a web navigation agent or a non-coding domain remains untested.\n\n- **Feature overlap ablation reveals the structural model is essentially an LF reconstructor.** On all OH windows, LF-overlapping features alone (11 features) outperform the full model (0.977 vs. 0.967). This means the 19 residual features actually hurt cross-agent performance. The paper interprets this as \"the features that generalize encode cross-agent stagnation signals,\" but the simpler interpretation is that the structural model's cross-agent success comes entirely from reconstructing the LF voting pattern, which happens to partially generalize across these two coding agents. The 0.977 number for LF-overlap-only is the most concerning finding in the paper and receives insufficient critical discussion.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. What is the cross-agent transfer performance of the structural GB model when trained on silver labels produced without $\\lambda_3$ (outcome backpropagation)? The 2-LF ablation in Table 3 shows the probe's performance degrades, but the structural model's performance under 2-LF labels is not reported for the deployment-relevant configuration.\n\n2. The gold set evaluation (GB 0.839, MV 0.798) is the paper's strongest non-circular evidence. What is the statistical power of this comparison? With N=505 and the observed effect size, what is the minimum detectable difference at 80% power?\n\n3. The feature overlap ablation shows LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent---exceeding the full model. How should readers interpret the \"structural features detect stagnation\" claim when 100% of cross-agent generalization (and more) comes from features that overlap with the labeling functions?\n\n4. How do the bootstrap confidence intervals change when adjusting for autocorrelation from stride-1 windowing? Even a simple effective-N correction using the autocorrelation function of consecutive window labels would help calibrate the reported CIs.\n\n5. The LLM judge evaluation reports GPT-5.5 with a 22.4% parse failure rate (originally 9.4% in the summary). Were failed parses treated as errors or excluded? This choice significantly affects the reported macro-F1.\n\n6. Why are the calibration, SHAP, power analysis, stride sensitivity, early termination, and stagnation tail analyses---all listed in the experiment plan and with scripts present in the repository---not included in the manuscript?\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The abstract claims \"0.967 vs. best LLM 0.799\" as a 16.8pp gap, but these are evaluated on different datasets (OH transfer vs. SWE-Agent in-domain for the LLM). This cross-context comparison should be flagged more prominently.\n- Table 1 footnote $^a$ notes the probe is evaluated on N=6,122 while structural models on N=150,652. This scope mismatch makes direct comparison in the same table misleading.\n- The paper title asks \"Are Stagnation Signals Structural?\" but the answer the paper provides is closer to \"Structural features reconstruct LF votes well, and LF votes partially transfer across agents.\" The title oversells the finding.\n- Some references have placeholder-style formatting (e.g., \"and others\" instead of proper author lists in the .bib file).\n- The claim that cmd_rep's value is \"purely coverage\" (100% agreement on overlapping windows) is somewhat misleading---it means cmd_rep never disagrees with the 2-LF consensus, but cmd_rep's high-precision votes on previously-unlabeled windows do add real information (otherwise random coverage expansion would also help).\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification:**\n\nThe paper tackles a relevant problem (detecting stagnation in LLM agents) and demonstrates commendable honesty about its circularity limitations. The ablation battery is extensive by the standards of empirical ML papers, and the negative result on temporal label models is a genuine contribution.\n\nHowever, several methodological concerns prevent acceptance at a top venue:\n\n(a) The headline result (0.967 cross-agent F1) is evaluated against silver labels that the structural features are designed to reconstruct. The feature overlap ablation confirms this: LF-overlapping features alone exceed the full model cross-agent. The paper acknowledges this but still foregrounds the silver-label metrics.\n\n(b) The gold validation---the paper's most credible evidence---is underpowered (N=505, kappa=0.39) and reveals a modest advantage (0.839 vs. 0.798) that the paper does not formally power-analyze. Several planned analyses (calibration, power analysis, stride sensitivity, SHAP, early termination simulation) that would strengthen the claims are absent from the manuscript despite having scripts in the repository.\n\n(c) The scope is narrow (2 coding agents on the same benchmark), the outcome-dependent LF creates a deployment gap that is not resolved, and the stride-1 autocorrelation is not quantified.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (1) foregrounding gold-set results as primary, (2) including the missing analyses (especially calibration, power, stride sensitivity), (3) demonstrating the pipeline works without $\\lambda_3$, and (4) being more explicit that the structural model's cross-agent success is primarily LF reconstruction that happens to partially generalize."}],"totalDurationMs":155349,"totalTokens":63577,"totalToolUseCount":24,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2682,"cache_read_input_tokens":57620,"output_tokens":3274,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2682},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3274,"cache_read_input_tokens":57620,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2682,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2682,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":14,"searchCount":9,"bashCount":1,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
